From 070daf905aed301c763e1b2472738890295d6855 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 05:07:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia-drm: a flip held until a time (NV_FLIP_TIME_RAW_NS),
 and Media VRR (NV_VRR_MEDIA)

A client that plays a video with VRR active (VRR_ENABLED) wants each picture to
start at its time: every 41.708 ms for 23.976 fps, or every 20.854 ms where
each picture goes twice (a display whose VRR starts at 40 Hz). With VRR active,
NVKMS starts a frame when a flip arrives, so a flip that leaves the CPU when a
timer fires starts its frame when the timer fired and the commit went through.

NVKMS can do better, and has the means already. The window channel of every
NVDisplay HAL (Volta on, so Turing to Blackwell) takes a timestamp with a flip
(validTimeStampBits 64, nvkms-evo3.c), and the flip of a layer with a non-zero
timeStamp is held by the display engine "until PTIMER >= timeStamp"
(NvKmsFlipCommonParams, SET_PRESENT_CONTROL TIMESTAMP_MODE). KAPI never set it.
Blackwell also has Media VRR: a refresh rate, fractional, that a flip asks for
and the display engine keeps by itself (NVKMS_REQUESTED_VRR_TYPE_MEDIA,
targetRefreshRate with its divider of 1.001, supportsVirtualRefreshRate).
KAPI never asked for it either.

Three properties of the CRTC:
- NV_FLIP_TIME_RAW_NS (every head): the earliest time at which the flip of the
  commit may be shown, in ns of CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW, the CPU clock of RM's
  GPU-CPU time correlation (the one NVKMS uses to turn the raster generator's
  timestamps into CPU time). KAPI turns it into PTIMER time with that
  correlation, read again once a second at most, and puts it in the main
  layer's timeStamp. It holds for one commit: a duplicated state has 0. A time
  more than a second from the correlation's reading, or a correlation that
  cannot be read, holds nothing: the flip goes out as before.
- NV_VRR_MEDIA (where the GPU has Media VRR): the mode set of the head asks
  for NVKMS_REQUESTED_VRR_TYPE_MEDIA instead of gaming VRR. A change of it is a
  mode set. Without VRR_ENABLED the head keeps the refresh rate of its mode,
  as before.
- NV_VRR_MEDIA_TARGET (likewise): the refresh rate that the flips ask for
  while VRR is active (Hz << 1, bit 0 for the divider of 1.001); NVKMS
  validates it against the display's VRR range.

Nothing changes for a client that does not set them: their defaults are 0,
and KAPI asks for gaming VRR, as before. Kodi's "Video at its own rate with VRR
(no change of mode)" sets them.

Checked: the five modules build against Linux 7.3.5 with the compiler of the
image; the patch applies with --fuzz=0 after the series before it, from
nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9072 to 9075. Not run on a GPU: whether the display engine starts a VRR frame at the time of a held flip,
and the offset of RM's correlation, are measured on hardware by the log of
Kodi's presentation.
